module

public import Sheaf.RingTheory.ChartCoordinateNonzerodivisors
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.AtPrime.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.Away.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.KrullDimension.Basic
import Mathlib.RingTheory.RegularLocalRing.Polynomial
import Mathlib.RingTheory.KrullDimension.Regular
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.Ideal
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Regular.Flat
import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.RegularLocalGeneratorsRegularSequence
import WeilConjectures.RingTheory.RegularLocalLocalization

/-!
# Simultaneous coordinate quotients in a parameter blowup chart

Source: Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, Expose VIII, Lemma 4.2.9,
printed p. 121, and Remark 4.2.2(i), printed p. 118
(blueprint: lem-trf-parameter-blowup-coordinate-quotients).

The chart and ratio coordinates are the existing `traceChartRing` and
`traceChartCoordinate`. The sum labels distinguish the exceptional equation,
ratio variables and unchanged parameters, even before evaluating their values.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

noncomputable section

universe u

namespace ParameterBlowupCoordinateQuotients

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R] {d : ℕ}

/-- The displayed labelled family, assembled from the existing chart coordinates.
`none` is the exceptional coordinate, `some (Sum.inl i)` a ratio coordinate, and
`some (Sum.inr k)` an unchanged parameter. Source: ILO VIII, Lemma 4.2.9, p. 121
(blueprint: lem-trf-parameter-blowup-coordinate-quotients). -/
def coordinate (t : Fin d → R) (J : Finset (Fin d)) (j : Fin d) :
    Option ((J.erase j) ⊕ {k : Fin d // k ∉ J}) → traceChartRing t J j
  | none => algebraMap R _ (t j)
  | some (Sum.inl i) => traceChartCoordinate t J j i.val i.property
  | some (Sum.inr k) => algebraMap R _ (t k.val)

/-- The ideal generated by a chosen subfamily of the labelled coordinates.
A subset of the sum labels is precisely a choice of epsilon, U and K; its cardinality
is epsilon + |U| + |K|. Source: ILO VIII, Lemma 4.2.9 and Remark 4.2.2(i), pp. 121, 118
(blueprint: lem-trf-parameter-blowup-coordinate-quotients). -/
def coordinateIdeal (t : Fin d → R) (J : Finset (Fin d)) (j : Fin d)
    (S : Finset (Option ((J.erase j) ⊕ {k : Fin d // k ∉ J}))) :
    Ideal (traceChartRing t J j) :=
  Ideal.span (coordinate t J j '' (S : Set _))

/-- Every simultaneous coordinate quotient is regular, with the expected local
codimension, and all vanishing labelled coordinates extend to one local parameter system.
A selected coordinate outside a prime gives a basic neighbourhood on which its
intersection ideal is the unit ideal.

Source: ILO VIII, Lemma 4.2.9, p. 121, and Remark 4.2.2(i), p. 118
(blueprint: lem-trf-parameter-blowup-coordinate-quotients).

Proof, following the seven steps of the node:
1. For chosen ratio indices U and outside indices K, put T = U union K. Reorder the
   original parameters with T first. By the regular-generator theorem
   (lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-generators-regular-sequence), R_T = R/(t_T) is regular
   local of dimension d - |T|. The other parameters generate its maximal ideal and
   have that length, hence are a regular system. Since j is not in T this dimension
   is positive. Killing u_U and t_K identifies the chart quotient with the chart B
   over R_T with variables indexed by L minus U: map coefficients to their classes,
   kill u_U, and retain the other variables. The inverse exists because t_i=t_j*u_i
   vanishes for i in U. Both composites fix coefficients and variables, hence are
   identities by def-weil-pbc-mv-polynomial-ring.
2. Apply lem-trf-parameter-blowup-algebra-regular to this parameter chart B. It is
   regular, multiplication by t_j is injective, and B[1/t_j] is R_T[1/t_j], sending
   u_i to t_i/t_j. Empty remaining variable families give B=R_T.
3. If the exceptional coordinate is also killed, the quotient instead is the
   polynomial ring over C=R/(t_h : h in J union K), on the surviving ratio variables.
   Coefficient quotient and variable substitution give inverse ring maps, since all
   original relations become zero. The same reordered-parameter theorem makes C
   regular local. By lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-localization it is regular, and
   lem-trf-polynomial-ring-regular-base makes its polynomial ring regular. This
   includes dimension-zero C and no surviving variables.
4. Injectivity of t_j makes B embed into B[1/t_j]: an element in the kernel is killed
   by a power of t_j, hence is zero by repeated cancellation. A remaining parameter
   t_i is a nonzerodivisor on R_T by putting it first in the remaining regular system.
   Inverting t_j preserves this: t_j^m*t_i*x=0 implies t_j^m*x=0. Thus t_i/t_j is a
   nonzerodivisor in R_T[1/t_j] and on B; the same argument applies to every remaining
   outside parameter. This uses def-weil-ring-localization-away and
   def-weil-pbc-regular-sequence.
5. Order the selected nonexceptional coordinates arbitrarily and append t_j last if
   selected. Every prefix quotient is a chart from step 1. Steps 2 and 4 prove the
   next multiplication injective, so this list is weakly regular. At a prime
   containing its ideal, lem-weil-pbc-weakly-regular-sequence-localization-prime makes
   it regular. The assumed regularity of the chart makes its prime localization
   regular local. By lem-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime-of-quotient its quotient is
   the prime localization of the regular ring from steps 2 and 3, hence regular local.
   Lemma lem-trf-regular-sequence-dimension-formula gives the dimension equality.
6. Select exactly the labels whose coordinates lie in a given prime. The regular
   quotient has dimension dim(S)-c by step 5. Choose a parameter system there and
   lift it to the maximal ideal of S. Together with the c selected equations these
   lifts generate that maximal ideal: reduce an element modulo the equations,
   express it in the quotient parameters, lift the coefficients, and subtract.
   Their length equals dim(S), so this is a regular system, with one distinct first
   position for every selected label. For c=0 choose a system of S; if the quotient
   dimension is zero no lifts are needed.
7. If a selected coordinate f is outside the prime, D(f) is a neighbourhood of the
   prime; in A[1/f] that equation is a unit, so the selected intersection ideal is top.
   Otherwise steps 5 and 6 supply its codimension and simultaneous local equations.
-/
theorem coordinate_quotients_and_parameters
    {R : Type u} [CommRing R] [IsRegularLocalRing R]
    (d : ℕ) (hd : 1 ≤ d) (hdim : ringKrullDim R = (d : WithBot ℕ∞))
    (t : Fin d → R) (ht : IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters (List.ofFn t))
    (J : Finset (Fin d)) (hJ : J.Nonempty) (j : Fin d) (hj : j ∈ J)
    [IsRegularRing (traceChartRing t J j)] :
    (∀ S : Finset (Option ((J.erase j) ⊕ {k : Fin d // k ∉ J})),
      IsRegularRing (traceChartRing t J j ⧸ coordinateIdeal t J j S) ∧
      ∀ p : PrimeSpectrum (traceChartRing t J j), coordinateIdeal t J j S ≤ p.asIdeal →
        IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal ⧸
          (coordinateIdeal t J j S).map
            (algebraMap (traceChartRing t J j) (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal))) ∧
        ringKrullDim (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal) =
          ringKrullDim (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal ⧸
            (coordinateIdeal t J j S).map
              (algebraMap (traceChartRing t J j) (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal))) +
            (S.card : WithBot ℕ∞)) ∧
    (∀ p : PrimeSpectrum (traceChartRing t J j),
      ∃ s : List (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal),
        IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters s ∧
        ∃ f : {a : Option ((J.erase j) ⊕ {k : Fin d // k ∉ J}) //
            coordinate t J j a ∈ p.asIdeal} → Fin s.length,
          Function.Injective f ∧
          ∀ a, s.get (f a) =
            algebraMap (traceChartRing t J j) (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal)
              (coordinate t J j a.val)) ∧
    (∀ (S : Finset (Option ((J.erase j) ⊕ {k : Fin d // k ∉ J})))
        (p : PrimeSpectrum (traceChartRing t J j)),
      (∃ a ∈ S, coordinate t J j a ∉ p.asIdeal) →
        ∃ a ∈ S, coordinate t J j a ∉ p.asIdeal ∧
          (coordinateIdeal t J j S).map
            (algebraMap (traceChartRing t J j) (Localization.Away (coordinate t J j a))) =
              ⊤) := by
  sorry

end ParameterBlowupCoordinateQuotients
